    "statement_raw_token": "\"Let $G$ be a bipartite graph with $n$ vertices. For a graph $G$, let $q_1(G),\\\\ldots,q_r(G)$ be its nonzero signless Laplacian eigenvalues, and define\\n\\n$$\\nS_\\\\alpha(G):=q_1(G)^\\\\alpha+\\\\cdots+q_r(G)^\\\\alpha.\\n$$\\n\\n**You and Yang's conjecture.** If $\\\\alpha>1$, then\\n\\n$$\\nS_\\\\alpha(G)\\\\leq n^\\\\alpha+\\\\left(\\\\lfloor n/2\\\\rfloor-1\\\\right)\\\\lceil n/2\\\\rceil^\\\\alpha+(\\\\lceil n/2\\\\rceil-1)\\\\lfloor n/2\\\\rfloor^\\\\alpha,\\n$$\\n\\nwith equality if and only if $G=K_{\\\\lfloor n/2\\\\rfloor,\\\\lceil n/2\\\\rceil}$.\\n\\nThis conjecture complements the known inequality for connected bipartite graphs when $0<\\\\alpha\\\\leq1$ and predicts that the balanced complete bipartite graph maximizes the signless Laplacian power sum for powers greater than one. Its validity is stated as open in the source.\"",
    "statement_raw_token_sha256": "43fbfe824085204659964df71ffe91febaf5825de7bd9cd45a9ffaede0dc8c3c",
